## Architecture rules

```text
App.vue (screens, settings) → Game (loop, rules, saving) → world / actors / gameplay / render / hud / audio
                                     ↑ content (src/content/demo.js)
```

- **`App.vue`** owns UI state and the `Game` instance: it creates a `Game` with fresh canvases (`canvasVersion` key), calls `init`, `resume`, `pause`, `applySettings` and `dispose`, and reacts to the callbacks `onPause`, `onComplete`, `onError`, `onSave`. It never reaches into engine modules.
- **`Game.js`** is the composition root and runs the only `requestAnimationFrame` loop: input → player/enemy → camera → renderer → HUD. Pixi must stay on `autoStart: false`; never add a second loop or timer-driven rendering.
- **Engine modules** (`src/game/**`) are independent of Vue and of the demo where possible; they must not import `App.vue` or touch the DOM outside their canvas. Content (level layout, positions, seeds) lives in `src/content/`.
- **Rules** that can be tested without WebGL go into pure functions (`gameplay/rules.js`, `SaveSystem.normalizeSave`, `Settings.normalizeSettings`).
- **Lifecycle:** everything a module creates — GPU geometry, materials, textures, render targets, listeners, audio nodes, timers — is released in its `dispose()`, and `Game.dispose()` calls them. A disposed WebGL canvas is never reused: App.vue replaces both canvases before creating a new `Game`.
- **Pausing:** losing focus, pointer lock or page visibility pauses the simulation and saves.
- **Errors:** failures during `init` or a frame are reported through `onError` and shown on the error screen; storage failures set the visible save warning. Never swallow an error silently unless the feature is optional (audio).
- **Saves** are browser-local (`localStorage`, keys `voxel.<APP_SLUG>.save.v1` and `voxel.<APP_SLUG>.settings.v1`), always written through `normalizeSave`/`normalizeSettings`. A save stores the level id, checkpoint, objectives and up to 2048 edited cells as numeric block ids.

## Commands
{% if choices.docker == "full" %}
**Everything runs in Docker.** Node.js is not needed on the host and `npm` must not be run there: `node_modules` lives in a Docker volume with Linux builds. Run every command below inside the dev container:

- `npm run dev` → `docker compose up dev` (Vite on {{ ports.WEB_PORT }}, published to the host);
- any other `npm …` → `docker compose run --rm dev npm …` (e.g. `docker compose run --rm dev npm run check`);
