## Configuration and environment

- One `.env` in the project root; `.env.example` lists every key with a comment and is committed. Never commit `.env`.
- The game title comes **only** from `APP_NAME`, and the save namespace from `APP_SLUG`. Code uses the build-time constants `__APP_NAME__` and `__APP_SLUG__` (defined in `vite.config.js`, declared as globals in `eslint.config.js`); `index.html` uses `%APP_NAME%`. Never hardcode the title or another project's storage keys.
- `.env` is read by `projectEnv()` in `scripts/env.mjs`, which keeps values literal (Vite's `loadEnv` would expand `$`). Never parse `.env` by hand elsewhere.
- To add a setting: add the key with a comment to `.env.example` and `.env`, read it in `vite.config.js` (build-time constant) or `scripts/serve.mjs` (production server), declare new globals in `eslint.config.js`, and document it in `README.md`. Everything defined for the browser is public: never put secrets there.
- Restart `npm run dev` after changing `.env`; rebuild for production.

## How to

### Change the demo level

Edit `src/content/demo.js`: `createDemo()` builds the world with `Gen` (heightmap, columns, flatten, forest, fill, set, protect, boundary). Positions in `DEMO` drive spawn, checkpoint, exit, enemy and crystals; `gameplay/rules.js` protects cells around them. **Change `DEMO.id`** (e.g. `meadow-v2`) whenever the generated terrain changes, because saves store edits as cell coordinates of that level and are ignored when the id differs. Update `tests/world.test.js` (deterministic spawn, bridge and crystal route).

### Add a block

1. Add a texture painter to `TEXTURES` in `src/game/world/Blocks.js` (helpers in `TexturePainter.js`), or reuse an existing key.
2. **Append** a `def('name', { … })` (shape, bucket, solid, light, sound, …) after the last block: saves store numeric ids, so never insert, reorder or remove blocks. At most 255 blocks.
3. Use it in content (`g.set`, `g.fill`) or make it placeable in `Game.editBlock()`.
4. Add a test in `tests/world.test.js` when it has new collision, light or meshing behaviour.

### Add a gameplay feature (objective, enemy, item)

1. Content (positions, counts) → `src/content/demo.js`.
2. Pure rules (reach, eligibility, protection) → `src/game/gameplay/rules.js` with tests in `tests/gameplay.test.js` or `tests/world.test.js`.
3. Runtime objects → a class in `src/game/gameplay/` (extend `Entity` for physics actors) created and disposed by `Game.js`, updated in `Game.frame()`.
4. Persistent state → add the field to `normalizeSave` (validate it, keep old saves loadable or bump `SAVE_VERSION`) with tests in `tests/save.test.js`.
5. Visible state → HUD (`Hud.js`) **and** `HudState.js`, or a screen in `App.vue`.

### Add a menu screen or setting

Screens are `screen` values in `App.vue` with a `<section>`; settings are fields of `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` and `normalizeSettings` in `src/game/core/Settings.js`, applied in `Game.applySettings()`. Add a test for the clamping in `tests/save.test.js`.

### Add input

Map a new action in `DEFAULT_BINDINGS` (and `PAD_MAP`) in `src/game/core/Input.js`, read it with `input.pressed('action')` / `held` in the game loop, and update the controls table in `README.md` and the pause screen.

## Testing

- Vitest runs in Node (`tests/*.test.js`); there is no browser or WebGL in tests. Test world logic, rules, saves and settings directly; for rendering code, test the work done (uploads, draw ranges, pass selection) with small fakes, as `tests/rendering.test.js` does.
- `tests/server.test.js` covers the production server and `.env` parsing (names with quotes, Cyrillic and `$`).
- Every new rule or bug fix gets a test. Interactive behaviour (controls, menus, rendering) must also be checked in a WebGL2 browser: new game, pause/resume, settings, continue and new-game replacement.

## Definition of done

1. `npm run check` passes with **zero** errors and warnings{% if choices.docker == "full" %} — run it as `docker compose run --rm dev npm run check`{% endif %}.
2. `npm run build` succeeds{% if choices.docker != "none" %} and `docker compose build app` still builds the image when `Dockerfile`, dependencies or runtime files changed{% endif %}.
3. Changed gameplay was tried in the browser (`npm run dev`) without console errors.
4. Docs match the code: `.env.example`, `README.md`{% if options.spec_md %}, `SPEC.md`{% endif %} and this file are updated when commands, env keys, controls, structure or rules change.

## Performance invariants

- Keep HUD invalidation in sync: every new visible HUD value must be part of `HudState.changed()`.
- Empty particle pools stay hidden, and buffer update ranges must cover every active particle after compaction or reuse.
- Reuse camera and animation scratch storage; never mutate authored animation clips.
- The mesher merges only opaque, stationary cube faces with identical encoded corner lighting and atlas regions. Preserve unit texture repeats, derivatives, AO gradients and material boundaries; compare against `new Mesher(world, atlas, { mergeFaces: false })` when changing it.
- Keep chromatic aberration on the separate output/grading passes. Do not lower resolution, effect density, shadow quality or animation cadence as a substitute for removing redundant work.

## Conventions

- ES modules with explicit `.js` extensions in imports; classes in `PascalCase.js`, helpers in `camelCase.js`.
- Units: world coordinates are blocks (1 unit = 1 block); character models use model pixels (`PX` in `Rig.js`). Angles are radians, colours are hex numbers.
- Deterministic generation: use `Rng`/`Noise` with the level seed, never `Math.random()` in world generation.
- All art and audio stay procedural or are added under `public/` with a clear licence; do not copy assets, names or characters from other games.
- Dependencies: prefer the platform and the existing libraries; add a package only when it clearly pays off and keep `package-lock.json` in sync (`npm ci` installs exactly it).
- Let Prettier format (`npm run format`); do not disable lint rules to get green.
- Write code, comments, UI texts and docs in English.
